WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.410
Welcome to Class 5: Slurm acceptance patterns.

2
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:07.794
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07.794 --> 00:00:14.614
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:17.050
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:23.829
GPU work follows the same principle: request the current GPU partition in a batch job and

6
00:00:23.829 --> 00:00:26.795
keep interactive GPU exploration attended and bounded.

7
00:00:26.795 --> 00:00:33.574
By the end of Class 5, you should also be able to distinguish a GPU partition,

8
00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:38.659
an exact GPU type, an architecture feature, host memory, and GPU memory.

9
00:00:38.659 --> 00:00:44.167
The GPU-selection lesson is included below rather than presented as a separate class.

10
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:49.689
The following screenshot was captured from the RCC scheduler on 25 July 2026.

11
00:00:49.689 --> 00:00:55.637
It shows what sinfo looks like, but the available nodes and partitions can change.

12
00:00:55.637 --> 00:00:58.610
Run sinfo yourself before choosing a partition.

13
00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:03.708
A minimal batch script is: Slurm normally allocates the requested CPU, memory,

14
00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:06.682
GPU, and time on a suitable node.

15
00:01:06.682 --> 00:01:10.930
It does not give the job an exclusive whole node.

16
00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:17.727
Ordinary jobs should not add --nodes or --exclusive; request whole or multiple nodes only for a

17
00:01:17.727 --> 00:01:20.276
measured application designed to use them.

18
00:01:20.276 --> 00:01:27.721
The default rule: Use the standard gpu_nodes partition and request one GPU without a model unless

19
00:01:27.721 --> 00:01:33.305
your software, memory requirement, or reproducibility plan genuinely requires a particular GPU.

20
00:01:33.305 --> 00:01:37.958
RCC does not create one queue for every GPU generation.

21
00:01:37.958 --> 00:01:45.404
Ampere, Blackwell, and future standard GPU systems can share gpu_nodes when their scheduling policy is the

22
00:01:45.404 --> 00:01:45.869
same.

23
00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:53.314
Slurm selects an exact model through a typed GPU request and an architecture through a node

24
00:01:53.314 --> 00:01:53.780
constraint.

25
00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.363
### GPU learning objectives After this part of Class 5, you can:

26
00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:06.809
distinguish a partition, GPU type, architecture feature, and system memory; discover the user-visible GPU labels without

27
00:02:06.809 --> 00:02:09.135
enumerating physical nodes; request any.

28
00:02:09.135 --> 00:02:15.732
The first job verifies scheduling, environment capture, output handling and exact comparison.

29
00:02:15.732 --> 00:02:20.839
The gate: submits only one job at a time; uses one CPU,

30
00:02:20.839 --> 00:02:27.648
128 MiB RAM and a two-minute limit; refuses job arrays; refuses to run when another learner

31
00:02:27.648 --> 00:02:32.755
gate is active for the same user; waits for a bounded period;

32
00:02:32.755 --> 00:02:39.564
compares output byte-for-byte; cleans only its own temporary directory; does not enumerate nodes or expose scheduler

33
00:02:39.564 --> 00:02:39.990
configuration.

34
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:45.780
A passing class gate shows that your account can execute the pattern.

35
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:53.019
It is not a cluster-wide health test and must not be expanded into host-by-host probing.

36
00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:59.775
Reference companion: After completing the bounded gates, use the Slurm command reference for dependencies,

37
00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:03.635
reusable allocations, GPU requests, accounting, cancellation, and checkpointing.

